p1_bogoliubov.py -- Phase 1 (agent PN): Shale-Stinespring HS norm E(s)=||P_- W_s P_+||_2^2
for the zero-collar recovery diffeomorphism of Note 5, extended to a diffeo of R.

CONVENTIONS (fixed once, used everywhere)
  Fourier:      fhat(k) = int_R f(x) e^{-i k x} dx,  f(x) = (1/2pi) int fhat(k) e^{ikx} dk
  Projections:  P_+ = 1_{k>0}  (Hardy space of the UPPER half plane), P_- = 1_{k<0}
  Push-forward of half-densities: (W_phi f)(y) = f(phi^{-1}(y)) ((phi^{-1})'(y))^{1/2},
                phi an increasing C^{1,1} diffeo of R.  W_phi is unitary on L^2(R).
  Displacement: u(x) := x - phi(x),  so phi' = 1 - u'.

====================================================================================================
(1) NORMALISATION AND VALIDATION
====================================================================================================
Identity used:  D := W* P_- W - P_-  has kernel K = (1/(2 pi i)) k(x,y),
   k(x,y) = sqrt(phi'(x)phi'(y))/(phi(x)-phi(y)) - 1/(x-y),  k(x,x)=0.
D = Q - P_- with Q = W* P_- W a projection, so Tr D^2 = Tr(Q P_+) + Tr(P_-(1-Q))
   = ||P_- W P_+||_2^2 + ||P_+ W P_-||_2^2 = 2 E.   Hence   E = (1/2)||K||_2^2 ,
   E = (1/(8 pi^2)) \iint k(x,y)^2 dx dy      <-- NOT ||K||_2^2 : the factor is 1/2.

 (a) Moebius maps: max|k| on (-2.9,40)^2 for phi=(2x+1)/(x+3):  1.76e-10
     (a Moebius map with a pole is not a diffeo of R; across the pole k = -2/(x-y),
      which is why only pole-free Moebius maps -- affine on R, or any Moebius map of
      the CIRCLE -- give E = 0.)   affine phi=1.7x+0.4:  E = 2.05e-27

 (b) phi = x + eps*exp(-x^2/2):  first order  E = eps^2 (1/(48 pi^2)) int_0^inf k^3|etahat|^2 dk
     = eps^2/(48 pi)   [Fourier convention fhat(k)=int f e^{-ikx}dx, P_+ = 1_{k>0}]
     eps      E (double integral)   E (matrix of P_-WP_+)   eps^2/(48 pi)    quad/exact
     0.02     2.65279701e-06        2.64233639e-06          2.65258238e-06   1.00008
     0.05     1.65870281e-05        1.65216460e-05          1.65786399e-05   1.00051
     0.1      6.64490227e-05        6.61874469e-05          6.63145596e-05   1.00203
     (matrix column: Fourier basis of a box [-X,X), Richardson-extrapolated in 1/X from
      X=50,100; it converges to the same value, confirming the factor 1/2 independently.)

 (c) circle picture (Cayley x = C tan(th/2), C=2).  P_-(th,th') = (1/(2 pi i))/(2 sin((th-th')/2)),
     kc = sqrt(psi'psi')/(2 sin(dpsi/2)) - 1/(2 sin(dth/2)),  E = (1/(8 pi^2)) \iint kc^2 dth dth'.
     eps=0.1 : E(line) = 6.6449022714e-05   E(circle) = 6.6449022714e-05
     eps=0.3 : E(line) = 6.0794547259e-04   E(circle) = 6.0794547259e-04

 (d) first-order (quadratic) functional, three independent evaluations for eta=exp(-x^2/2):
     real-space kernel  0.0066314560 | Fourier int k^3|etahat|^2 0.0066314560 | circle (1/12)sum(n^3-n)|etat_n|^2 0.0066314560
     => Q[g] = (1/(48 pi^2)) int_0^inf k^3 |ghat|^2 dk = (1/12) sum_{n>=2}(n^3-n)|gt_n|^2 ,
        gt = g * dtheta/dx the same vector field in the circle chart (Moebius invariant).

====================================================================================================
(2) THE FAMILY (Note 5: a=1, L=2, zeta = a s/(a+L) = s/3)
====================================================================================================
k~_s(x) = x on (-inf,0], = L x/(L+s x) on [0,L]; u_s = x - k~_s = s x^2/(L+s x) on [0,L],
u_s(L)=sL/(1+s), u_s'(L)=s(2+s)/(1+s)^2.  The extension is FREE on I' = (-inf,-a] u [L,inf),
i.e. on the single circle arc J' through infinity; it must match u_s,u_s' at L and vanish
to first order at -a.  Parametrisation (circle chart): psi = th - delta, delta = cubic
Hermite on J' matching (delta,delta') at both ends, minus lam*s*sum_i c_i sin^2(pi u)
{cos,sin}(m pi u), u=(th-th_L)/|J'|, m=0..M; the c_i minimise the first-order functional.
NOTE: the optimal extension moves the point at infinity (it is asymptotically affine on R,
      NOT decaying to the identity).  Restricting to extensions with u -> 0 at |x| = inf
      gives min 0.5 E/zeta^2 = 0.0678 (M=90, still falling slowly), a factor 8.0 too
      large -- see section (4) and finding PN-2.

